    "narrative": "Today\u2019s roster for **LawDog** (7641) leans heavily into favorable platoon splits and high-value spots in the order against vulnerable arms. **Lawrence Butler** (71.26) and **Bryson Stott** (71.23) share the top honors today; **Lawrence Butler** benefits from a high-run-environment park and a strong platoon edge against P\u00e9rez, while **Bryson Stott** exploits the high xERA of Avila and is positioned well to capitalize on the 50% SB potential against a pitcher who struggles to hold runners. **Taylor Ward** (71.19) is our premier leverage play, as he draws a significant boost from facing the left-handed Lodolo, maximizing his power ceiling in a favorable hitting environment. We are also monitoring **Jung Hoo Lee** (61.69) closely; while his base projection is modest, his 100% stolen base profile against a \"slow delivery\" pitcher makes him a critical asset in our quest to win the scarcest category in this 5x5 format.\n\nThe most difficult decision at the bottom of the active roster was opting to bench **Drew Gilbert** (50.86). Despite his park-adjusted upside against Gordon, the combination of his low batting order position and the high efficiency scores of **Daulton Varsho** (52.12) and **Gavin Sheets** (51.06) left **Drew Gilbert** on the outside looking in. We continue to manage around the absence of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who remains on the IL, and we are tracking **Yainer Diaz** and **Tyler Freeman** as high-upside bench depth should late-day lineup changes occur. While **Geraldo Perdomo** (59.18) sits near the marginal threshold, his favorable spot in the order keeps him active as we prioritize keeping our remaining game caps in check. Aside from **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, our main risk factor remains the Lodolo matchup for **Jeremiah Jackson**, where a low spot in the order suppresses his otherwise decent raw projection.",
